CertHub.register({"id":"az-104","vendor":"Microsoft","name":"Microsoft Certified: Azure Administrator Associate","short":"Azure Administrator","exam":"AZ-104","blurb":"For administrators who run an organization's Azure environment day to day: Entra ID users and groups, RBAC and governance, storage, virtual machines, containers and App Service, virtual networking, monitoring, backup and recovery.","status":"check","statusNote":"AZ-104 is active, and no retirement or replacement had been announced as of Sept 24, 2026 (search results for Microsoft's 2026 retirement list, which removed AZ-204 and AZ-500, do not include AZ-104). Skills measured, as shown in search excerpts of the Microsoft Learn AZ-104 study guide: Manage Azure identities and governance 20–25%; Implement and manage storage 15–20%; Deploy and manage Azure compute resources 20–25%; Implement and manage virtual networking 15–20%; Monitor and maintain Azure resources 10–15%. Midpoints (22.5/17.5/22.5/17.5/12.5 = 92.5) were scaled to 100 and rounded to 24/19/24/19/14. learn.microsoft.com is blocked by this environment's network proxy, so the study guide could not be opened directly: re-check the ranges, the change log and the exam length on the study guide before booking. Third-party sites report a skills-measured refresh on Sept 17, 2026 (Container Apps alongside Container Instances, more weight on identity and governance); this has not been confirmed on Microsoft Learn.","lastVerified":"2026-09-24","notices":[{"from":"2026-09-24","until":"2026-12-31","text":"Third-party sites report that Microsoft refreshed the AZ-104 skills measured on Sept 17, 2026. Domain ranges appear unchanged, but open the change log at the end of the official study guide and compare it with this plan before you book."}],"examInfo":{"questions":"Not published (Microsoft associate exams usually have 40–60 questions, which can include case studies)","minutes":100,"pass":"700 on a 1–1000 scale","extra":"Certification must be renewed every year with a free online renewal assessment on Microsoft Learn. Microsoft recommends hands-on experience with the portal, PowerShell, Azure CLI and ARM templates or Bicep."},"examSim":{"questions":50,"minutes":100},"sources":[{"label":"Microsoft Learn: Study guide for Exam AZ-104 (skills measured)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/credentials/certifications/resources/study-guides/az-104"},{"label":"Microsoft Learn: Azure Administrator Associate certification","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/credentials/certifications/azure-administrator/"},{"label":"Microsoft Learn: Course AZ-104T00 Microsoft Azure Administrator","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/courses/az-104t00"}],"planWeeks":10,"hoursPerWeek":"6–8","domains":[{"id":1,"name":"Manage Azure identities and governance","w":24,"topics":["Microsoft Entra users and groups: create, bulk create, security vs Microsoft 365 groups, assigned vs dynamic membership","User and group properties, licenses (including group-based licensing), external (B2B guest) users and self-service password reset","Azure RBAC: built-in roles (Owner, Contributor, Reader, User Access Administrator), assigning roles at different scopes and interpreting access","Entra roles vs Azure RBAC roles, and data-plane roles such as Storage Blob Data Reader","Azure Policy: definitions, initiatives, assignments, scopes and exclusions, effects (Deny, Audit, Modify, DeployIfNotExists) and remediation tasks","Resource locks: CanNotDelete vs ReadOnly, inheritance and who can remove them","Tags: applying tags, why tags are not inherited, and enforcing or inheriting tags with policy","Resource groups: create, move resources between groups and subscriptions","Subscriptions and management groups: hierarchy, inheritance of policy and RBAC","Cost management: cost analysis, budgets and budget alerts, Azure Advisor cost recommendations, reservations"],"notes":["Skills measured: Manage Microsoft Entra users and groups; Manage access to Azure resources; Manage Azure subscriptions and governance"],"labs":["Free Azure account: in Microsoft Entra ID create two users and a security group, add a guest user by email, then assign the group Reader on a new resource group and sign in as one user to confirm what they can and cannot change. Entra ID Free covers this at no cost.","Free Azure account: create a budget of $5 on your subscription with an email alert at 50%, then assign the built-in policy 'Allowed locations' to a resource group and try to create a storage account in another region to see the Deny. Policy and budgets are free.","Free Azure account: add a CanNotDelete lock to a resource group containing only an empty storage account, try to delete it, remove the lock, then delete the group. Cost warning: delete the resource group when done so nothing keeps billing after your free credit."]},{"id":2,"name":"Implement and manage storage","w":19,"topics":["Storage account types and redundancy: LRS, ZRS, GRS, RA-GRS, GZRS and RA-GZRS","Storage firewalls and virtual network rules, trusted Microsoft services, private endpoints for storage","Shared access signatures: account, service and user delegation SAS; stored access policies; access keys and key rotation","Identity-based access for Azure Files (AD DS, Entra Domain Services, Entra Kerberos) with share-level RBAC and NTFS permissions","Encryption: Microsoft-managed vs customer-managed keys in Key Vault, infrastructure encryption, encryption scopes","Object replication, and data movement with AzCopy and Azure Storage Explorer","Blob containers and access tiers: Hot, Cool, Cold and Archive; rehydration from Archive","Lifecycle management policies that tier or delete blobs by age","Data protection: blob and container soft delete, versioning, snapshots, change feed","Azure Files: create and configure file shares, snapshots, soft delete, and SMB port 445 considerations"],"notes":["Skills measured: Configure access to storage; Configure and manage storage accounts; Configure Azure Files and Azure Blob Storage"],"labs":["Free Azure account: create a Standard LRS StorageV2 account, upload a blob with Storage Explorer, generate a read-only SAS that expires in 1 hour and open the file in a private browser window; then regenerate key1 and confirm the SAS stops working.","Free Azure account: enable blob versioning and soft delete, overwrite and delete a file, then restore the old version; add a lifecycle rule that moves blobs to Cool after 30 days and deletes them after 365.","Free Azure account: install AzCopy and copy a local folder into a container with `azcopy copy` using a SAS, then create a 5 GiB Azure file share and mount it (Windows needs outbound TCP 445). Cost warning: storage is cheap but not free after the credit, so delete the account afterward."]},{"id":3,"name":"Deploy and manage Azure compute resources","w":24,"topics":["ARM templates and Bicep files: interpret and modify, deploy, export a deployment as a template, convert ARM JSON to Bicep","Deployment modes (incremental vs complete) and deploying with `az deployment group create` or `New-AzResourceGroupDeployment`","Create virtual machines: images, sizes, OS and data disks, disk types (Standard HDD to Ultra), encryption at host and Azure Disk Encryption","Resize VMs, move VMs between resource groups, subscriptions and regions, manage disks","Availability sets (fault and update domains) vs availability zones and their SLAs","Virtual Machine Scale Sets: orchestration modes, autoscale rules, scale-in","Azure Container Registry tiers and image management","Azure Container Instances (container groups, restart policies) and Azure Container Apps (revisions, ingress, scale rules)","App Service plans: tiers, scaling up vs scaling out, autoscale","App Service: TLS certificates, custom DNS names, backups, networking (VNet integration, private endpoints) and deployment slots"],"notes":["Skills measured: Automate deployment of resources by using ARM templates or Bicep files; Create and configure virtual machines; Provision and manage containers; Create and configure Azure App Service"],"labs":["Free Azure account: write a 15-line Bicep file that creates a storage account with a name parameter, deploy it with `az deployment group create`, then export the resource group template in the portal and compare it with your file. Cost warning: delete the resource group afterward.","Free Azure account: create a B1s Linux VM (covered by the free 12-month allowance, 750 hours a month), SSH in, attach a small data disk, resize the VM and stop (deallocate) it. Cost warning: a VM that is only shut down from inside the OS keeps billing compute; deallocate it and delete the resource group, including the public IP and disks, when finished.","Free Azure account: create a Free (F1) App Service web app, deploy the sample from the portal quickstart, then run `az container create` with the public hello-world image and browse to its IP. Cost warning: deployment slots need Standard or higher, which uses credit; delete everything the same day."]},{"id":4,"name":"Implement and manage virtual networking","w":19,"topics":["Virtual networks and subnets: address space planning, the 5 reserved IPs per subnet","Virtual network peering: non-transitive, gateway transit and use remote gateways, global peering","Public IP addresses: Standard SKU, static allocation, zones","User-defined routes: route tables, next hop types (virtual appliance, virtual network gateway, internet, none) and forced tunneling","Network security groups and application security groups: rule priority, default rules, subnet vs NIC association, effective security rules","Azure Bastion: AzureBastionSubnet, SKUs, browser and native client access","Service endpoints vs private endpoints, and private DNS zones for private link","Azure DNS: public zones, delegation, record sets, alias records; private DNS zones with auto-registration","Azure Load Balancer: public vs internal, Standard SKU, backend pools, health probes, load-balancing and inbound NAT rules","Troubleshooting connectivity with Network Watcher: IP flow verify, next hop, connection troubleshoot, effective routes"],"notes":["Skills measured: Configure and manage virtual networks in Azure; Configure secure access to virtual networks; Configure name resolution and load balancing"],"labs":["Free Azure account: create two VNets with non-overlapping ranges (10.1.0.0/16 and 10.2.0.0/16), peer them, deploy a B1s VM in each and ping between them; check the effective routes on one NIC. Cost warning: delete both VMs and their public IPs afterward.","Free Azure account: add an NSG to a subnet that denies inbound TCP 22 from the internet, then use Network Watcher IP flow verify to prove the rule blocks SSH and that a new higher-priority allow rule from your IP opens it.","Free Azure account: create a private DNS zone (lab.internal) linked to a VNet with auto-registration, deploy a VM and resolve its name from another VM with `nslookup`. Cost warning: Azure Bastion and Standard Load Balancers bill by the hour, so if you try them, delete them as soon as you finish."]},{"id":5,"name":"Monitor and maintain Azure resources","w":14,"topics":["Azure Monitor metrics vs logs, and diagnostic settings that send resource logs to a Log Analytics workspace","Querying logs with basic KQL (where, summarize, project, render)","Alert rules (metric, log search, activity log), action groups and alert processing rules","Azure Monitor insights: VM insights, storage and network insights, Azure Monitor Agent and data collection rules","Network Watcher and Connection Monitor","Recovery Services vault vs Backup vault and what each protects","Backup policies (standard and enhanced), on-demand backup, soft delete and cross-region restore","Restoring VMs, disks and individual files","Azure Site Recovery: replication to a secondary region, test failover, failover, commit and failback","Backup reports and alerts"],"notes":["Skills measured: Monitor resources in Azure; Implement backup and recovery"],"labs":["Free Azure account: create a Log Analytics workspace, send a VM's data with the Azure Monitor Agent through a data collection rule, and run a KQL query that shows the last hour of heartbeats. Cost warning: Log Analytics ingestion beyond the free 5 GB a month is billed, so collect only a few counters.","Free Azure account: create a CPU metric alert on a VM above 80% for 5 minutes with an action group that emails you, run a stress command to trigger it, then add an alert processing rule that suppresses notifications for the next hour.","Free Azure account: create a Recovery Services vault in the VM's region, back up a B1s VM on demand, restore a single file with File Recovery, then stop backup and delete the backup data. Cost warning: protected instances and snapshots bill after the free credit; soft delete keeps data 14 days."]}],"study":{"1":[["What is the difference between Microsoft Entra roles and Azure RBAC roles?","Entra roles (Global Administrator, User Administrator) control the directory: users, groups, apps and licenses. Azure RBAC roles (Owner, Contributor, Reader) control Azure resources at management group, subscription, resource group or resource scope. They are assigned separately."],["Contrast Owner, Contributor, Reader and User Access Administrator.","Owner has full access including role assignments. Contributor can create and manage all resources but cannot grant access. Reader can only view. User Access Administrator manages role assignments but not the resources themselves."],["How do dynamic groups work and what do they need?","Membership is calculated from a rule on user or device attributes (for example department equals Sales). Members are added and removed automatically. Dynamic membership needs Microsoft Entra ID P1 or higher licensing."],["When would you use Azure Policy instead of RBAC?","RBAC controls who can do something; Policy controls what can be deployed or how resources must be configured, whoever deploys them. For example, Policy can deny regions or require tags even for an Owner."],["Compare the two lock types and explain inheritance.","CanNotDelete allows reading and changes but blocks deletion. ReadOnly allows reading only and can break operations that use POST calls, such as listing storage keys. Locks apply to the scope and everything under it, and the most restrictive lock wins."],["How do you keep costs under control in a subscription?","Use Cost Management cost analysis to see spend by resource group or tag, create budgets with alerts and action groups, act on Azure Advisor cost recommendations (right-size or shut down idle VMs), and use reservations or savings plans for steady workloads."]],"2":[["Explain LRS, ZRS, GRS and GZRS.","LRS keeps three copies in one datacenter. ZRS keeps three copies across availability zones in one region. GRS adds asynchronous copies to a paired secondary region (six in total). GZRS combines ZRS in the primary with a copy in the secondary. RA- versions let you read from the secondary."],["Compare account SAS, service SAS and user delegation SAS.","An account SAS is signed with the account key and can cover several services. A service SAS is signed with the key and covers one service. A user delegation SAS is signed with Entra credentials, works for Blob Storage only and is the most secure choice."],["How can you revoke a SAS before it expires?","Link service SAS tokens to a stored access policy and change or delete the policy; or regenerate the account key used to sign it (which breaks every SAS and app using that key); for a user delegation SAS, revoke the user delegation keys."],["Describe the blob access tiers and their trade-offs.","Hot has the highest storage but lowest access cost. Cool (30-day minimum) and Cold (90-day minimum) trade cheaper storage for higher access cost. Archive (180-day minimum) is cheapest but offline: blobs must be rehydrated, which can take hours."],["How do you give domain users NTFS-style access to Azure Files?","Enable identity-based authentication (AD DS, Entra Domain Services or Entra Kerberos), assign share-level RBAC roles such as Storage File Data SMB Share Contributor, then set Windows ACLs on folders and files. Clients mount over SMB 445."],["What does object replication require?","Blob versioning on both source and destination accounts and change feed on the source. It copies block blobs asynchronously between containers according to rules."]],"3":[["What is the difference between incremental and complete deployment mode?","Incremental (the default) adds or updates resources in the template and leaves others in the resource group alone. Complete deletes resources in the resource group that are not in the template, so it must be used carefully."],["Compare availability sets and availability zones.","An availability set spreads VMs across fault domains (racks) and update domains in one datacenter, giving a 99.95% SLA. Availability zones place VMs in physically separate datacenters within a region, giving 99.99% and protecting against a datacenter failure."],["Why might resizing a VM require it to be deallocated?","If the new size is not available on the hardware cluster hosting the VM, Azure must move it to different hardware, which requires stopping (deallocating) it first."],["When would you use Container Instances vs Container Apps?","Container Instances run a single container group quickly with no orchestration, suited to simple tasks and batch jobs. Container Apps add revisions, ingress, traffic splitting and event-driven autoscaling (including scale to zero) for microservices."],["Explain deployment slots and slot settings.","Slots are live apps with their own host names (Standard tier and higher). You deploy to staging, warm it up, then swap with production for near-zero downtime and easy rollback. Settings marked as deployment slot settings stay with the slot instead of swapping."],["Scale up vs scale out for an App Service plan?","Scale up changes the plan tier or size for more CPU, memory and features. Scale out adds instances, manually or with autoscale rules (Standard tier and higher)."]],"4":[["Why is VNet peering described as non-transitive?","If A peers with B and B peers with C, A cannot reach C through B. You need a direct peering or a hub with a network virtual appliance or gateway and user-defined routes."],["How are NSG rules evaluated?","Rules are processed by priority, lowest number first, and processing stops at the first match. For inbound traffic the subnet NSG is checked and then the NIC NSG; both must allow the traffic. Default rules (65000 and above) allow VNet and load balancer traffic and deny the rest."],["Compare service endpoints and private endpoints.","A service endpoint keeps the service's public endpoint but lets you restrict it to traffic from chosen subnets over the Azure backbone. A private endpoint gives the service a private IP in your VNet, reachable from peered and on-premises networks, and needs private DNS."],["What does Azure Bastion give you and what does it need?","Browser-based RDP and SSH to VMs over TLS without public IPs on the VMs. It is deployed in a subnet named AzureBastionSubnet (/26 or larger) and uses a Standard public IP."],["What components make up an Azure Load Balancer configuration?","A frontend IP (public or private), a backend pool of VMs or scale set instances, health probes that decide which backends receive traffic, and load-balancing rules (plus optional inbound NAT and outbound rules)."],["How do you delegate a domain to Azure DNS?","Create a public DNS zone for the domain in Azure, note its four Azure name servers, and update the NS records at the domain registrar to point to them."]],"5":[["Metrics or logs: which would you use and when?","Metrics are numeric time series, near real time and cheap, good for fast alerts on CPU or latency. Logs are detailed records in a Log Analytics workspace, queried with KQL for investigation and correlation."],["What are action groups and alert processing rules?","An action group defines who is notified and what runs (email, SMS, webhook, Logic App, runbook) when an alert fires. An alert processing rule adds or suppresses action groups on fired alerts, for example to silence notifications during maintenance."],["Recovery Services vault or Backup vault?","Recovery Services vaults protect Azure VMs, Azure Files, SQL Server and SAP HANA in VMs and on-premises workloads through MARS or MABS. Backup vaults protect newer workloads such as Azure managed disks, blobs and Azure Database for PostgreSQL."],["What is the difference between Azure Backup and Azure Site Recovery?","Backup keeps point-in-time copies to restore after data loss or corruption. Site Recovery continuously replicates VMs to another region so you can fail over the whole workload during an outage, with a short RPO."],["Why run a test failover in Site Recovery?","It brings up replicated VMs in an isolated network without affecting production or replication, proving the recovery plan, network settings and application order work before a real disaster."]]},"qCount":90});
